@STATIC_LINKED@
Your rights
-----------

- You may replace any of these DLLs with a modified version of the same library, as long as your
  build stays binary compatible with the one shipped: 64-bit (x64), built with the Microsoft
  Visual C++ compiler against the dynamic C runtime, and the same library version. Qt's modules
  and plugins also use each other's internal interfaces, so replace Qt files with a build of the
  same Qt version and configuration. Copy your build over the file of the same name in the
  installation folder. Lucent does not check signatures or hashes of these DLLs and does nothing
  to prevent their replacement.
- Lucent's terms do not restrict modifying these libraries for your own use, or reverse
  engineering Lucent for debugging such modifications.
- The licence texts are included in this folder:
@LICENCE_TEXTS@

Corresponding source
--------------------

The libraries were built, without changes by the Lucent project, from the upstream source archives
listed below, with the build recipe (build script and patches) of the vcpkg port named for each.
vcpkg verifies every archive against the SHA-512 value shown. A recipe is identified by the git
tree hash of its port folder in the vcpkg registry (https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg) and, when
the registry commit Lucent builds against (@VCPKG_BASELINE@) holds exactly that tree, by
a link to the port folder at that commit. A port that Lucent overlays (vcpkg's recipe with
Lucent's build changes, in the cmake/ports folder of Lucent's source) is identified by that
folder at the commit this installation was built from. The same information is available in
machine-readable form in Lucent.spdx.json (SPDX 2.3).

